1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,147
Picture a grassland in what is now Texas, a

2
00:00:03,147 --> 00:00:06,294
few million years ago. The sun is going down.

3
00:00:06,294 --> 00:00:09,440
A small animal freezes in the grass, because something

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:12,587
tall and very still is watching from the edge

5
00:00:12,587 --> 00:00:13,636
of the field.

6
00:00:13,986 --> 00:00:16,636
It's a bird. But not the kind you'd feed

7
00:00:16,636 --> 00:00:19,286
at a park. It stands taller than a grown

8
00:00:19,286 --> 00:00:21,936
man's shoulders, and its head is built like a

9
00:00:21,936 --> 00:00:23,703
heavy hammer with a hooked beak.

10
00:00:24,053 --> 00:00:27,203
Its name was Titanis walleri. And for a long

11
00:00:27,203 --> 00:00:30,354
time, scientists weren't sure what it was doing with

12
00:00:30,354 --> 00:00:31,054
that beak.

13
00:00:31,404 --> 00:00:34,472
Today we're going back to the Pliocene epoch, roughly

14
00:00:34,472 --> 00:00:37,540
2 to 5 million years ago. We'll look at

15
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:40,608
what this bird was, where its fossils were found,

16
00:00:40,608 --> 00:00:43,676
how it may have lived, and why researchers still

17
00:00:43,676 --> 00:00:45,040
argue about its diet.

18
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:48,461
Let's start with the name. The genus name Titanis

19
00:00:48,461 --> 00:00:51,533
comes from the Titans of Greek myth, which is

20
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:54,604
a fair hint about how big scientists thought it

21
00:00:54,604 --> 00:00:57,675
was. The species name honors Benjamin Waller, the fossil

22
00:00:57,675 --> 00:01:00,746
hunter who found its first bones in Florida's Santa

23
00:01:00,746 --> 00:01:01,429
Fe River.

24
00:01:01,779 --> 00:01:05,540
Titanis stood about 1.9 meters tall, roughly 6 feet,

25
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:08,048
when measured from foot to head.

26
00:01:08,398 --> 00:01:11,378
For a US audience, think of a basketball player

27
00:01:11,378 --> 00:01:14,357
standing next to the bird. The two could look

28
00:01:14,357 --> 00:01:17,337
each other in the eye. Its body was heavy,

29
00:01:17,337 --> 00:01:20,316
but not built for hovering or gliding. Its legs

30
00:01:20,316 --> 00:01:23,296
were long and powerful, made for running across open

31
00:01:23,296 --> 00:01:23,627
ground.

32
00:01:23,977 --> 00:01:27,486
Here's the detail that changes everything: Titanis could not

33
00:01:27,486 --> 00:01:30,995
fly. Its wings were far too small for lift.

34
00:01:30,995 --> 00:01:34,504
Everything about this animal was built for the ground.

35
00:01:34,854 --> 00:01:37,848
The skull is what makes Titanis famous. It had

36
00:01:37,848 --> 00:01:40,842
a massive, hooked beak, and the bones around it

37
00:01:40,842 --> 00:01:43,836
were thick and strong. The shape suggests a bird

38
00:01:43,836 --> 00:01:46,165
that could strike hard and hold on.

39
00:01:46,515 --> 00:01:49,411
But a beak tells us what an animal could

40
00:01:49,411 --> 00:01:52,306
do, not what it did. That's where the debate

41
00:01:52,306 --> 00:01:52,628
begins.

42
00:01:52,978 --> 00:01:55,841
The first fossils of Titanis were found in Florida,

43
00:01:55,841 --> 00:01:58,385
and more have since turned up in Texas.

44
00:01:58,735 --> 00:02:01,706
This is important for a US audience, because it

45
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:04,678
means this bird lived in places many people walk

46
00:02:04,678 --> 00:02:07,650
through today. The grasslands it hunted looked different then,

47
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:10,621
but the land and the rivers were still familiar.

48
00:02:10,971 --> 00:02:14,106
This is the question people ask most. Did Titanis

49
00:02:14,106 --> 00:02:17,240
hunt its own food, or did it scavenge the

50
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:19,330
remains of animals killed by others?

51
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:23,150
Scientists have argued both sides. A strong beak and

52
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:26,620
powerful legs suggest hunting. But the same features could

53
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:29,319
also help a scavenger open a carcass.

54
00:02:29,669 --> 00:02:32,385
So the honest answer is that we don't know

55
00:02:32,385 --> 00:02:35,101
for certain. The bird may have done both, depending

56
00:02:35,101 --> 00:02:37,816
on the season, the food, and the other animals

57
00:02:37,816 --> 00:02:38,420
around it.

58
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:42,019
Today we have birds that run fast and fight

59
00:02:42,019 --> 00:02:45,268
hard, like ostriches and cassowaries. Titanis is part of

60
00:02:45,268 --> 00:02:48,518
a larger group of ground birds called phorusrhacids, sometimes

61
00:02:48,518 --> 00:02:51,767
called "terror birds." Many of them were in South

62
00:02:51,767 --> 00:02:55,016
America, and Titanis is one of the few that

63
00:02:55,016 --> 00:02:56,821
made it to North America.

64
00:02:57,171 --> 00:03:00,253
That is a remarkable story. A group of birds

65
00:03:00,253 --> 00:03:03,336
that spread across the land bridge into North America,

66
00:03:03,336 --> 00:03:06,418
and one of them became the Texas terror bird.

67
00:03:06,768 --> 00:03:10,540
Titanis disappeared, and no single cause has been proven.

68
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:14,312
Scientists point to changes in climate, vegetation, and the

69
00:03:14,312 --> 00:03:16,407
other animals in the ecosystem.

70
00:03:16,757 --> 00:03:19,744
What's striking is how much the world can change,

71
00:03:19,744 --> 00:03:22,399
even for a bird this big and powerful.

72
00:03:22,749 --> 00:03:25,622
So the next time you're in a field in

73
00:03:25,622 --> 00:03:28,495
Texas, remember that this was once a place where

74
00:03:28,495 --> 00:03:31,368
a bird taller than most people walked the grass.

75
00:03:31,368 --> 00:03:34,241
It wasn't a monster, just a very large animal

76
00:03:34,241 --> 00:03:37,114
with a very strange beak, living in a world

77
00:03:37,114 --> 00:03:38,710
we only know from bones.

78
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:41,618
If you want Part 3, where we go deeper

79
00:03:41,618 --> 00:03:44,175
into the Texas fossils and compare them with other

80
00:03:44,175 --> 00:03:46,733
terror birds, let me know in the comments. And

81
00:03:46,733 --> 00:03:49,290
if you want to see the next predator, watch

82
00:03:49,290 --> 00:03:50,711
the next one right here.
